WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.680
QUESTION: So I have been hearing about this project (Pleistocene Park) for many many years.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.780
You guys are finally there and you're seeing it
and you're with George (Church) and other scientists...

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:14.600
I guess my question is did it seem
more or less feasible?

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.620
More or less plausible?
More or less achievable?

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.260
And what kind of resources are going to be required?

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.760
Stewart Brand: That's a perfect question.
Because I was going there looking for that.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:35.520
And part of it is the sheer competence
of the Zimovs. Both of them.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:42.400
And the depth of time and commitment they've
made there. That they've got the beginnings of

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.460
a real-life demo of... what had been (only) an essay
in Science magazine some while back.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.460
When you have a  density of animals on the land
it really starts to change

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.240
in the way that one would be glad of.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.360
And there's always more animals.
Basically the land coming back to life.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.760
But that sheer competence they have. I mean...

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.100
They're in such an impossible situation.
All day every day.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.380
From out standpoint.
The fact that they can somehow... I mean

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.980
Nikita has stories to tell of getting through
the snow and almost dying...

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.460
Being in a situation where he's lost. He's not sure
that he's going in the right direction.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.520
He's frozen... they all have stories like this!

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.040
So these are insanely competent characters.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:33.400
And I trust their science. And I trust
their capability to carry it through.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.420
And a completely different level in the lab.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.820
I feel the same way about George Church.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:46.000
So this particular project is in such good hands.
And the science think is so well proven at this point

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.660
that I believe it will all go forward.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:53.820
Alexander Rose: I think for me was it was
both sides of this equation.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:02:00.560
Both I was stunned to find out how realistic it was
to do the basic science of the project.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.980
And I was also stunned at
how massive the scale of the problem is,

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.740
and how immediate that tundra melting problem is.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.060
It was literally the summer we were there, and

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:19.280
the week we were there, the paper was published
around the frozen layer: huge portions of it not freezing.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:26.780
And after you fly 10 hours across Siberia,
you're like, wow, there's a lot of stuff to do here.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.820
Stewart Brand: So if you can look out if all that
landscape you've been flying over for 10 hours

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.340
and think: all of this landscape is
becoming a greenhouse gas problem.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.840
And might be converted over time
into a greenhouse gas solution.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.380
That's big. And it doesn't even include
northern North America yet.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:47.080
But it will. 
Kevin Kelly: I don't have much doubt that

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:55.420
a mammoth will be resurrected. And that it even
could have the effect of clearing Pleistocene Park.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:03.360
I think it's another level of belief to imagine
it spreading across all Siberia.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.800
I mean, it could.
But that's just another question.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.780
So I think there's different levels of success.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:17.320
And I think... the close success of making
a version of a mammoth

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:23.960
and having it have an effect within that area,
I think that looks like it kinda could really happen.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.670
Whether it takes off or whether it has the total effect

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.837
everybody wants, I think that's much more uncertain.

00:03:31.837 --> 00:03:38.240
But I think the first thing seems very plausible to me.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.660
Stewart Brand: Jason and David what did you think?

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:46.960
David Alvarado: Yeah... I mean, you're asking a couple of Millennials if we can edit genes. And I think, absolutely!

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.940
You know, to us it's natural, right?

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.320
We just grow up in the era of seeing computers change, rapid rapid rapid...

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.340
And so this feels very natural, part of
like our timeline of witnessing technology

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.160
I think for us as filmmakers...

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.600
the thing that we know our audience is going
to be asking is that sort of Jurassic Park question.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.980
"You spent too much time thinking whether you could do it, that you didn’t stop to think if you should!"

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:13.500
Slam the table. Well to us there's
so much more interesting ideas there.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.590
Should we? And what does it mean when humans
both decide to do it, to go forward with that,

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:22.960
or if we decide not to, we choose
not to use technology to

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.320
try to safeguard the planet,
I think there's just so much there that

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.280
there's not clear answers.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.600
But it should be talked about.

